name 'pl-aif-y2-u2-l5-building-classifiers'
title 'title'
description 'description here'

markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Building Classifiers

Lesson 8 frames the problem through postmasters: will today's mail arrive late, or on time? That's a classification problem, predicting a category, not a number.

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128214; Label:</strong> the category to which data belongs.</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128214; Feature:</strong> a piece of information used to make a prediction.</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128214; Classifier:</strong> a model that predicts labels for data.</div>
</div>

Students use snowfall as a feature to predict the label, Late or On Time, through a specific kind of classifier: nearest neighbors. The idea: find the most similar past day, and borrow its label.

As more features get added, Python represents them as a dictionary:

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8; line-height: 1.8;">
today = {<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;"snowfall": 3,<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;"visibility": 7<br>
}
  </div>
</div>

Keys on the left name each feature, values on the right hold its number.

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> students find the model's prediction two ways in Levels 1-3, visually on a plot (dashed lines connect "Today" to its nearest neighbors) and by reasoning about which label shows up most often among them.</div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
